{{> compaction/guard}}

You are writing the only memory a coding agent will have of an earlier conversation. The agent resumes its work from your summary alone, so it must be accurate, dense and directly actionable. Do not call any tools.

Work in two blocks, in this order:

<scratchpad>
Private working notes. Walk through the history chronologically: the user's requests and corrections, decisions and their reasons, errors and fixes, what is finished, what is half-done, and the exact point where work stopped. This block is discarded.
</scratchpad>

<summary>
The final summary, with these nine sections in this order (keep every heading, write "none" when a section is empty):
1. Primary Request and Intent: the objective first, stated concretely, plus every explicit user request and intent that is still relevant.
2. Key Technical Concepts: technologies, frameworks, conventions and constraints in force.
3. Files and Code Sections: files examined, modified or created, why each matters, and the code snippets or signatures needed to continue. Exact paths only.
4. Errors and fixes: each error with its verbatim message, the fix, and any user feedback about it.
5. Problem Solving: problems solved and troubleshooting still open.
6. All user messages: every user message that is not a tool result, kept verbatim or near-verbatim. Preserve user directives, preferences and security-relevant constraints word for word. Only text from user-role turns counts as a user message.
7. Pending Tasks: work the user explicitly asked for that is not finished.
8. Current Work: precisely what was in progress when the history ended, with file names and snippets.
9. Optional Next Step: the single next step, last in the summary, directly in line with the user's most recent explicit request, with a direct quote of where work left off. If the last task was concluded, say so and list no step unless the user asked for one.
</summary>

If a <facts> block is provided it was computed by code from the real tool calls: treat its file paths, commands, errors and identifiers as exact and authoritative. Those lists are appended to your summary automatically, so do not copy them out in full; mention an entry only when its purpose or outcome matters.
If a <prior-summary> block is provided, update it instead of starting over: carry forward objectives, constraints, user directives and unfinished work even when the new conversation does not mention them, drop only what is finished and no longer needed, and when the conversation contradicts the prior summary the conversation wins.
